<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-sheafiness">
  <title>Sheafiness</title>
  <p>
    We isolate the elementary descent statement used to transfer sheafiness from the three comparison rings.
  </p>
  <lemma xml:id="lem-sheaf-transfer">
    <title>Sheaf transfer</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
<me>\begin{matrix}
R&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;C\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
B&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;D
\end{matrix}</me>
be a commutative square of complete Tate Huber pairs.  For <m>E\in\{B,C,D\}</m>, write
<me>p_E:X_E=\Spa(E,E^+)\longrightarrow X_R=\Spa(R,R^+)</me>
for the induced map.  For a rational domain <m>U\subset X_R</m>, put
<m>U_E=p_E^{-1}(U)</m>, and set <m>U_R=U</m>.  Suppose that for every rational domain <m>U\subset X_R</m> the section rings satisfy a natural strict exact sequence
        <men xml:id="eq-sheaf-transfer-row">0\longrightarrow \cO_R(U)\longrightarrow
  \cO_B(U_B)\oplus\cO_C(U_C)
  \longrightarrow\cO_D(U_D)\longrightarrow 0.</men>
        If the Huber pairs associated with <m>B,C,D</m> are sheafy as complete topological rings, then the Huber pair associated with <m>R</m> is sheafy as a complete topological ring.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        We first work on the rational basis.  Let
<m>U=\bigcup_{i=1}^nU_i</m> be a finite rational covering.  For <m>E\in\{R,B,C,D\}</m>, let
<me>r_E:\cO_E(U_E)\longrightarrow\prod_i\cO_E((U_i)_E)</me>
denote product restriction, using <m>U_R=U</m> and <m>(U_i)_R=U_i</m> as specified in the statement.  The first map in
<xref ref="eq-sheaf-transfer-row"/> is a topological embedding, and the same is true after replacing <m>U</m> by any <m>U_i</m>.  Since <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are sheafy, <m>r_B</m> and <m>r_C</m> are topological embeddings.  Naturality gives a commutative square
<me>\begin{matrix}
\cO_R(U)&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;\cO_B(U_B)\oplus\cO_C(U_C)\\
\downarrow r_R&amp;&amp;\downarrow r_B\oplus r_C\\
\prod_i\cO_R(U_i)&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
\prod_i\bigl(\cO_B((U_i)_B)\oplus\cO_C((U_i)_C)\bigr).
\end{matrix}</me>
The top and right arrows are topological embeddings, as is the bottom arrow.  It follows that <m>r_R</m> is a topological embedding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now let <m>x_i\in\cO_R(U_i)</m> be a matching family.  Its images in the <m>B</m>- and <m>C</m>-section rings glue to elements
<me>b\in\cO_B(U_B),
  \qquad
  c\in\cO_C(U_C).</me>
Their images in <m>\cO_D(U_D)</m> agree after restriction to every <m>(U_i)_D</m>.  Since the structure presheaf for <m>D</m> is separated, they agree globally.  Exactness on <m>U</m> therefore gives
<m>x\in\cO_R(U)</m> mapping to <m>(b,c)</m>.  Exactness on each <m>U_i</m> shows that
<m>x|_{U_i}=x_i</m>, and uniqueness follows in the same way.  Thus <m>r_R</m> identifies
<m>\cO_R(U)</m> homeomorphically with the matching-family equalizer, which is closed in the finite product because all section rings are Hausdorff.
      </p>
      <p>
        The underlying presheaf is therefore a sheaf by the sheaf-on-a-basis theorem
<xref ref="bib-Stacks" detail="Tag 009O"/>.  To retain the topology on arbitrary opens, let <m>V=\bigcup_\lambda V_\lambda</m> be an open covering and let <m>M</m> be its matching-family equalizer with the subspace topology.  For every rational <m>U\subset V</m>, quasi-compactness gives a finite rational cover of <m>U</m> subordinate to the <m>V_\lambda</m>.  Restriction from <m>M</m> to that finite cover, followed by the rational-basis result, gives a continuous map <m>M\to\cO_R(U)</m>, independent of the chosen subordinate cover.  These maps are compatible under rational restriction.  Since
<me>\cO_R(V)=\varprojlim_{U\subset V,\ U\ \mathrm{rational}}\cO_R(U)</me>
with its projective-limit topology, the inverse of the algebraic gluing map <m>\cO_R(V)\to M</m> is continuous.  Hence the gluing map is a homeomorphism, as required.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-sheafy">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The Huber pair <m>(A,A^\circ)</m> is sheafy.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.isSheafy_JetA_of_dvr">Lean (isSheafy_JetA_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_isSheafyFor_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyFor_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_isSheafyTateRing_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyTateRing_of_dvr)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_structurePresheaf_isSheafOfTopologicalRings_all_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_structurePresheaf_isSheafOfTopologicalRings_all_of_dvr)</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Give <m>B,C,D</m> their maximal rings of integral elements.  The max/Gauss estimates <xref ref="eq-structural-map-bounds"/> show that every structural map is norm-nonincreasing.  Consequently it sends bounded subsets to bounded subsets; in particular, if <m>x</m> is power-bounded, then the set of powers of its image is the image of the bounded set <m>\{x^n:n\geq0\}</m>.  The structural maps therefore send power-bounded elements to power-bounded elements and define morphisms of Huber pairs.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a rational domain <m>U\subset\Spa(A,A^\circ)</m>, its inverse image in each comparison spectrum is defined by the image of the same rational datum.  By
<xref ref="prop-localized-milnor"/>, the corresponding section rings form the natural exact sequence required in <xref ref="lem-sheaf-transfer"/>.  Each of <m>B,C,D</m> is an affinoid <m>k</m>-algebra, hence is sheafy by Huber's theorem <xref ref="bib-Huber94" detail="Theorem 2.2"/>.  The conclusion follows from <xref ref="lem-sheaf-transfer"/>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  <remark xml:id="rem-pushout-diagram">
    <title>Affinoid versus global pushouts</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The coefficient descriptions of the maximal plus rings give
<me>A^\circ=B^\circ\times_{D^\circ}C^\circ.</me>
Indeed, a compatible pair which is power-bounded in <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> has its constant and linear <m>Q</m>-coefficients in both <m>k\langle W\rangle</m> and <m>L_0</m>; their intersection is <m>k^\circ\langle W\rangle</m>, while the higher <m>Q</m>-tail is already integral in <m>C_0</m>.  Together with the pullback topology, this says that the defining square is a pullback of complete Huber pairs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Put <m>X_E=\Spa(E,E^\circ)</m> for <m>E\in\{A,B,C,D\}</m>.  Huber's affinoid mapping theorem identifies morphisms between affinoid adic spaces with continuous morphisms of the corresponding complete Huber pairs <xref ref="bib-Huber94" detail="Proposition 2.1"/>.  Hence, for every affinoid adic space <m>Z</m>, the natural map
<me>\operatorname{Hom}(X_A,Z)\longrightarrow
 \operatorname{Hom}(X_B,Z)
 \times_{\operatorname{Hom}(X_D,Z)}
 \operatorname{Hom}(X_C,Z)</me>
is bijective.  Thus the contravariant square
<me>\begin{matrix}
X_D&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;X_C\\
\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
X_B&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;X_A
\end{matrix}</me>
is a pushout in the full subcategory of affinoid adic spaces.
      </p>
      <p>
        This does not make it a pushout in the category of all adic spaces: there the same map must be bijective for an arbitrary, possibly nonaffinoid, target <m>Z</m>.  In fact, uniqueness already fails for an explicit nonaffinoid target.  Let
<me>U_1=\{x\in X_A:|Q^2(x)|\leq |\varpi(x)|\},\qquad
 U_2=\{x\in X_A:|1|\leq |W(x)|\},\qquad U=U_1\cup U_2.</me>
The map <m>X_B\to X_A</m> factors through <m>U_1</m>, because <m>Q^2=0</m> in <m>B</m>, while <m>X_C\to X_A</m> factors through <m>U_2</m>, because both <m>W</m> and <m>W^{-1}</m> are power-bounded in <m>C</m> and hence <m>|W|=1</m> on <m>X_C</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The open subset <m>U</m> is proper.  Use additive valuations with values in <m>\Gamma=\mathbf Z^3_{\mathrm{lex}}</m> and, for a nonzero restricted series in the support description <xref ref="eq-monomial-support"/>, put
<me>v\left(\sum c_{a,b}W^aQ^b\right)
 =\min_{\mathrm{lex}}\{(v_k(c_{a,b}),b,a):c_{a,b}\neq0\}.</me>
Restrictedness makes the minimum exist, and the initial-term argument for the lexicographic monomial order makes <m>v</m> multiplicative.  The support condition makes it nonnegative on <m>A_0=A^\circ</m>, while <m>v(\varpi)>0</m>, so it is continuous and defines a point <m>x\in X_A</m>.  Since
<me>v(Q^2)=(0,2,0)&lt;(1,0,0)=v(\varpi),\qquad v(W)=(0,0,1)>0,</me>
one has <m>|Q^2(x)|>|\varpi(x)|</m> and <m>|W(x)|&lt;1</m>; hence <m>x\notin U</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Adic spaces may be glued along open subspaces.  Glue two copies of <m>X_A</m> along <m>U</m> and write <m>Z=X_A^{(1)}\cup_U X_A^{(2)}</m>.  The two open immersions <m>j_1,j_2:X_A\to Z</m> agree on <m>U</m>, so they agree after precomposition with both <m>X_B\to X_A</m> and <m>X_C\to X_A</m>, but they are distinct at the two unglued copies of <m>x</m>.  The displayed Hom map is therefore not injective for this <m>Z</m>.  Thus <m>X_A</m> is not the pushout in the full category of adic spaces.  More generally, the same doubling argument shows that a cocone whose two legs factor through a proper open subspace of its vertex cannot be a pushout in that category.  We do not know whether the original span admits some other, necessarily nonaffinoid, pushout object.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <title>Proof of <xref ref="thm-main"/></title>
      <p>
        Completeness, uniformity, nonnoetherianity, and the domain property are
<xref ref="prop-uniform-domain"/>; sheafiness is
<xref ref="thm-sheafy"/>; and failure of stable uniformity is
<xref ref="prop-not-stably-uniform"/>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </remark>
</section>
